---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {i18nSidebar} from '../../../data/i18n-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout title="Kraft Integration" sidebar={i18nSidebar}
            description="Reactive translations in Kraft — I18nController, the Translations delegate, form validation">

    <h1>Kraft Integration</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        Switch language, and every subscribed component re-renders.
    </p>

    <h2>Register a controller</h2>

    <p><code>I18nController</code> is a <code>Stream&lt;I18n&gt;</code>. Register it on the app and the whole
        component tree can reach it.</p>

    <Code code={`val kraft = kraftApp {
    semanticUI()
    i18n(
        I18nController.create(
            locale = Locale("en"),
            fallback = Locale("en"),
        ) {
            install(AppI18nCatalog)
        }
    )
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p><code>create</code> persists the chosen language in localStorage (key
        <code>kraft.i18n.lang</code> by default). Boot precedence is: the stored value, else
        <code>initialLang</code>, which itself defaults to the browser's language. Use
        <code>I18nController.inMemory(...)</code> for tests and anywhere you do not want persistence.</p>

    <p>Kraft's own form catalog is <strong>always installed first</strong>, at the lowest precedence, so
        validation messages translate even if an app forgets to provide anything. App catalogs install
        afterwards and win on shared keys.</p>

    <h2>Using translations in a component</h2>

    <Code code={`class GreetingComponent(ctx: Ctx<Props>) : Component<Props>(ctx) {

    private val t by Translations

    override fun VDom.render() {
        h1 { +t.checkout.greeting(name = props.name) }
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>The <code>by Translations</code> delegate subscribes to the controller's translation stream, so the
        component re-renders when the language changes and unsubscribes when it unmounts. There is a matching
        <code>by Formatting</code> delegate for the formatting surface.</p>

    <p>If no controller is registered, <code>by Translations</code> <strong>degrades to returning the key</strong>
        rather than crashing. A component in a test harness, or an app that has not wired i18n yet, still
        renders.</p>

    <h2>Switching language</h2>

    <Code code={`i18nController.setLang("de")`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>The controller maps the chosen tag through <code>Locale.parse</code> onto a fresh <code>I18n</code>
        snapshot and emits it. Every subscribed component re-renders in the new language, and the choice is
        persisted.</p>

    <h2>Form validation</h2>

    <p>Kraft's form rules resolve their messages through the same surface, so validation errors follow the
        selected language with no extra wiring:</p>

    <Code code={`UiInputField(value, onChange = { value = it }) {
    label("Email")
    accepts(notBlank(), isEmail())
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Rules produce their message via the i18n surface rather than a hardcoded string, and an app overrides
        any of them by defining the same key in its own catalog &mdash; app catalogs install last and win.</p>

    <Aside>Form rules were the first thing wired up, deliberately. They are the highest-volume user-facing
        strings in an app and the ones most likely to be seen in anger, so if the mechanism were awkward it
        would have shown up immediately.
    </Aside>

    <h2>Async rules</h2>

    <p>Validation rules may be suspending &mdash; a uniqueness check against the server, for example. The
        form controller exposes a status so the UI can disable submit while checks are in flight, and a guard
        so a form cannot be submitted while validation is still running.</p>

    <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <a href="/ultra/kraft/"
           class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-brand-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-brand-500 transition-colors">
            Kraft documentation &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
